---
// VISUAILS — FigFlow: the whole production pipeline as one continuous rail,
// with controls the reader can actually turn. Augustus 2026.
//
// WAAROM DIT BESTAAT. Lucas stuurde vijf schermafbeeldingen van een
// betaalproduct — een verticale rail waarlangs geld naar beneden loopt, met
// onderweg een kaart vol schakelaars, een slot, en onderaan een splitsing naar
// drie valuta's en drie merchants — en schreef: *"Ik zou iets als dit willen
// maar dan meer interactief maar 1 opzicht de gehele production pipeline, wel
// creatief aanpassen naar hoe dit verwerkt kan worden met het visuails
// systeem, dit is namelijk een betalingssysteem."*
//
// Twee opdrachten in één zin. "Eén opzicht" is een structuurprobleem: /how-it-
// works vertelde het verhaal in VIJF losse figuren met tekst ertussen, en wie
// het geheel wilde zien moest het zelf in zijn hoofd aan elkaar plakken.
// "Meer interactief" is het verschil met de referentie: dáár schuiven de
// schakelaars vanzelf om terwijl je scrollt — het is een film van een product.
// Hier zijn het echte schakelaars.
//
// WAT DE VIJF FIGUREN BEWEERDEN, EN WAAR DAT NU ZIT. Dit is geen samenvatting
// maar een verhuizing: elke claim die een van de vijf maakte en die de tekst
// eromheen niet ook maakte, moest mee. Ze stonden er niet voor niets.
//
//   FigFanOut  — "één foto erin, een reeks eruit", plus de bekentenis dat drie
//                van de vier uitvoerframes GETEKEND zijn en niet gefotografeerd.
//                → stage 1 en de fan-out onderaan; de bekentenis staat in de
//                  figcaption die de pagina meegeeft (`note`).
//   FigPipeline — dat stap 2 geen stap is maar een POORT: een ding met twee
//                kanten dat werk kan weigeren. Een genummerd kaartje gaf de
//                weigering evenveel gewicht als "je stuurt ons foto's".
//                → de gate-node, getekend als een deuropening, met de
//                  weigerkant zichtbaar ernaast.
//   FigCapacity — allebei de uitkomsten tegelijk, inclusief de verliezende. En
//                de harde regel uit die figuur: er mag NIETS te tellen zijn.
//                Een schaal op de banden is een leveringsbelofte die niemand
//                heeft gedaan. → de twee sporen bij de gate hebben geen maat.
//   FigApproval — PARALLELLISME: goedgekeurd, in revisie en nog onderweg naast
//                elkaar, want een opsomming is een plaatje van het tegendeel.
//                Plus: dit is een tekening, geen schermafbeelding.
//                → de portal-node met drie tegels in drie toestanden.
//   FigFormats  — dat één beeld vijf kanaalvormen overleeft, en dat elk frame
//                de verhouding IS waar het label onder staat (echte
//                `aspect-ratio`, geen met de hand gekozen breedte).
//                → de fan-out onderaan, met dezelfde vijf ratio's in dezelfde
//                  volgorde.
//
// DE CREATIEVE VERTALING. In de referentie loopt er geld door de rail en
// bepalen de schakelaars wat er met dat geld gebeurt. Hier loopt er WERK door
// de rail. De schakelaars bepalen de scope, en de schuif bepaalt het aantal
// producten — en dat laatste is de vondst. De prijsladder van VISUAILS is het
// moeilijkst uit te leggen deel van het aanbod ("het tarief daalt naarmate het
// aantal stijgt") en een tabel laat dat zien als vijf regels waar je zelf je
// eigen geval in moet opzoeken. Een schuif die je van 1 naar 40 sleept terwijl
// het bedrag onder je duim van €149 naar €55 loopt, is diezelfde ladder als
// beweging. En hij doet nog iets: bij tien producten springt de poort eronder
// om van wachtrij naar gereserveerd venster, want dat IS wat er bij tien
// gebeurt. Eén bedieningselement laat de prijs, de doorlooptijd, de
// leveringsvorm en de poort tegelijk kantelen, omdat die vier in het echte
// systeem ook aan dezelfde knop hangen.
//
// GEEN TAAL IN DIT BESTAND. Alle copy komt als prop uit de tweetalige COPY-
// tabel van de aanroepende pagina — dezelfde regel als bij de vijf figuren die
// dit vervangt, zodat EN/NL-pariteit op één plek te controleren blijft. De
// enige tekens die hier hardgecodeerd staan zijn cijfers en ratio-labels
// ("1:1"), en die zijn in beide talen gelijk.
//
// GEEN ARROW FUNCTIONS IN JSX-ATTRIBUTEN, GEEN GEÏNTERPOLEERDE CLASSNAMEN.
// Zie de kop van FigPipeline: de `>` in `=>` breekt tag-scanners, en een
// classnaam die als `` `is-${x}` `` wordt gebouwd staat noch in de markup noch
// in de CSS waar een checker hem kan vinden. Alles wat afgeleid is, wordt
// hierboven in de frontmatter uitgerekend.
//
// ZONDER JAVASCRIPT MOET DIT KLOPPEN. DESIGN.md's regel is dat animatie een
// al-zichtbare toestand versiert. Daarom rendert de server de figuur in een
// echte, complete begintoestand — catalog en lifestyle aan, video uit, twaalf
// producten — en zijn de bedieningselementen gewone `<input>`s. Zonder script
// kun je ze omzetten en gebeurt er niets; wat je ziet blijft een waar plaatje
// van een bestelling van twaalf producten. Met script rekent alles mee.
//
// DE PRIJZEN KOMEN NIET UIT DIT BESTAND EN OOK NIET UIT DE CLIENT-CODE. De
// ladder staat in src/data/pricing.js en mag daar staan. De pagina rekent één
// keer per taal ELKE mogelijke uitkomst uit — zeven schakelaarstanden × 40
// aantallen — en zet die als JSON in de pagina. Het script zoekt op, het rekent
// niet. Dat is bewust: een tweede implementatie van de ladder in browser-JS is
// precies hoe twee prijzen ontstaan die vandaag toevallig gelijk zijn.
//
// En de sleutel is de STAND VAN DE SCHAKELAARS, niet de naam van een ladder.
// De eerste versie sleutelde op laddernaam en liet de browser zelf uitrekenen
// dat catalog + lifestyle de `complete`-trede is — waarmee die regel op twee
// plekken stond, en ze waren het meteen oneens: lifestyle uitzetten wisselde
// het tarief naar catalog maar liet de regel "en elk product zakt naar €109"
// van de vorige stand eronder staan. Elke stand heeft nu een eigen rij, dus er
// is geen regel om te verdubbelen en geen toestand die kan verschalen.

interface Channel {
  /** The channel this crop is for — "Shop listing", "Reel or story".
   *  No `alt` field: FigFormats needed one because its frames were crops of a
   *  real photograph and each was separate evidence. These are drawn boxes
   *  whose whole content is the ratio label inside them, which is text. */
  t: string;
}
interface Source {
  /** Shot name — "Front", "Back". */
  t: string;
  /** A real photograph, when one exists for this slot. Drawn otherwise. */
  src?: string;
  alt?: string;
}
interface Scope {
  /** 'catalog' | 'lifestyle' | 'video' — the toggle's identity. */
  id: string;
  /** "Catalog set" */
  t: string;
  /** "4 images per product" */
  b: string;
  /** Whether it starts switched on. */
  on?: boolean;
}
interface Props {
  /** Stage headings, in order, from the page's COPY table. */
  stages: { send: string; scope: string; gate: string; make: string; deliver: string };
  /** The four shots the customer sends. */
  sources: Source[];
  /** The three scope toggles. */
  scopes: Scope[];
  /** Label + unit for the product count control. */
  countLabel: string;
  countUnit: string;
  /** Gate copy for both sides of the doorway. */
  gatePass: string;
  gateRefuse: string;
  gateNote: string;
  /** The two production nodes. */
  makeSteps: { t: string; b: string }[];
  /** Portal tile states — approved, in revision, still coming. */
  portalStates: { ok: string; rev: string; wait: string };
  /** What the delivery is called at each tier. */
  deliverPortal: string;
  deliverLink: string;
  /** The five channel crops. */
  channels: Channel[];
  /** Per-count strings the script swaps in. Built by the page from pricing.js. */
  rates: Record<string, { rate: string; total: string; per: string; next: string | null }>;
  /** Timing strings per tier, from turnaround() — never a literal. */
  timing: { attended: string; unattended: string };
  /** The count at which the tier flips. */
  threshold: number;
  /** Highest count the slider reaches. */
  maxCount: number;
  /** Where the slider starts, and what the server renders. */
  startCount: number;
  /** "excl. VAT" — from vatLabel(), never typed here. */
  vat: string;
  /** Small print under the whole figure. Carries the drawn-not-photographed
   *  disclosure and the not-a-screenshot one. */
  note: string;
  /** Accessible name for the figure as a whole. */
  label: string;
  /** "Two more products and every product drops to …" — shown when a next
   *  rung exists. The page supplies the template already filled per count in
   *  `rates`, so this is only the fallback label for the region. */
  nextLabel: string;
  /** What stands where the price was when every switch is off. */
  emptyLabel: string;
  /** "{n} images leave the studio" — one string with a {n} hole in it, so the
   *  number can sit anywhere in the sentence the language wants. */
  imagesLabel: string;
}

const {
  stages, sources, scopes, countLabel, countUnit,
  gatePass, gateRefuse, gateNote, makeSteps, portalStates,
  deliverPortal, deliverLink, channels, rates, timing,
  threshold, maxCount, startCount, vat, note, label, nextLabel, emptyLabel,
  imagesLabel,
} = Astro.props;

// Split on the {n} hole. A page that forgets the hole gets its sentence with
// the number appended rather than a crash — the sentence is still true, it is
// just badly worded, and that is the right failure for a caption.
const imagesParts = String(imagesLabel).split('{n}');
const imagesBefore = imagesParts[0];
const imagesAfter = imagesParts.length > 1 ? imagesParts[1] : '';

// The five ratios, in the order FigFormats published them. Coupled to
// `channels` by index exactly as FigFormats coupled RATIOS to `frames`: the
// caption promises each frame IS its ratio, so the pair must not drift. Kept
// as a literal here rather than imported because FigFormats is being retired
// and a constant whose only remaining reader is this file belongs in this file.
const RATIOS = [
  { r: '1 / 1', label: '1:1' },
  { r: '4 / 5', label: '4:5' },
  { r: '9 / 16', label: '9:16' },
  { r: '16 / 9', label: '16:9' },
  { r: '3 / 4', label: '3:4' },
];

// The scope toggles' initial state, and the image count that follows from it.
// IMAGES_PER is the same arithmetic pricing.js documents beside the ladder —
// a catalog set is four, a lifestyle carousel is three, a clip is one — and it
// is repeated here rather than imported because it is not a price and pricing.js
// does not export it as a number. If those set sizes ever change, this is the
// second place to look; the first is PER_PRODUCT's `line` copy.
const IMAGES_PER = { catalog: 4, lifestyle: 3, video: 1 };
const startOn = scopes.filter(function isOn(s) { return s.on !== false; }).map(function id(s) { return s.id; });
const startImages = startOn.reduce(function add(n, id) { return n + (IMAGES_PER[id] || 0); }, 0);

// The rate table is keyed by the literal switch state — "110:12" is catalog on,
// lifestyle on, video off, twelve products — so the server builds its key the
// same way the browser will. No ladder rule lives on either side; see the note
// above flowRates in HowItWorksPage.astro for why that mattered.
function bit(id) { return startOn.indexOf(id) > -1 ? '1' : '0'; }
const START_KEY = bit('catalog') + bit('lifestyle') + bit('video') + ':' + startCount;

// The server-rendered state. Everything below reads from these, so the no-JS
// page is internally consistent rather than a set of defaults that happen to
// sit near each other. The fallback is not defensive padding: `scopes` with
// everything switched off is a state the reader can reach in one click, and it
// has nothing to quote.
const BLANK = { rate: '', per: '', total: '', next: null };
const start = rates[START_KEY] || BLANK;
const startPriced = !!rates[START_KEY];
const startAttended = startCount >= threshold;
const startTiming = startAttended ? timing.attended : timing.unattended;
const startDelivery = startAttended ? deliverPortal : deliverLink;

// The config the client script reads. Prices are pre-formatted strings, one per
// possible count — see the header note on why no ladder arithmetic ships to the
// browser. `imagesPer` is here too so the script does not re-declare it.
const config = {
  threshold, maxCount, startCount,
  imagesPer: IMAGES_PER,
  rates,
  timing,
  delivery: { attended: deliverPortal, unattended: deliverLink },
};
---

<figure class="fl" aria-label={label} data-flow>
  <script type="application/json" data-flow-config set:html={JSON.stringify(config)} />

  <div class="fl-rail">

    {/* ── 1 · WHAT YOU SEND ─────────────────────────────────────────────
        The four shots, converging. In the reference this row is three buyer
        avatars; here it is the folder the customer drops, which is the actual
        input and the thing the H1 above promises. */}
    <section class="fl-stage" data-flow-stage="send">
      <h3 class="fl-h">{stages.send}</h3>
      <ul class="fl-sources">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li class="fl-src">
            {s.src
              ? <img src={s.src} alt={s.alt} width="480" height="480" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
              : <span class="fl-src-drawn" aria-hidden="true"></span>}
            <span class="fl-src-t">{s.t}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {/* Four lines meeting one. This was a single rounded rectangle in the
          first pass, which drew a box under the tiles rather than a junction —
          it did not touch the tiles above it or the rail below it, so it read
          as a stray frame. Mirrored deliberately against the split at the foot
          of the figure: the same drawing, run the other way, is what makes
          "four things in, one order" and "one order, five shapes" read as two
          halves of one movement rather than two unrelated diagrams. */}
      <span class="fl-converge" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 500 80" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
          <path d="M62 0 V16 Q62 40 110 40 H226 Q250 40 250 64 V80" />
          <path d="M187 0 V16 Q187 40 211 40 H238 Q250 40 250 64 V80" />
          <path d="M312 0 V16 Q312 40 288 40 H262 Q250 40 250 64 V80" />
          <path d="M437 0 V16 Q437 40 389 40 H274 Q250 40 250 64 V80" />
        </svg>
      </span>
    </section>

    {/* The travelling token. The reference runs a € down a dotted line; the
        thing travelling here is the order itself, so the token is a frame. */}
    <span class="fl-flow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fl-token"></span></span>

    {/* ── 2 · SCOPE — THE ONE INTERACTIVE CARD ──────────────────────────
        Real inputs, not painted ones. The reference's toggles flip themselves
        as you scroll, which looks alive and answers nothing; these answer
        "what would mine cost", which is the question the page exists for. */}
    <section class="fl-stage" data-flow-stage="scope">
      <h3 class="fl-h">{stages.scope}</h3>
      {/* THE CONTROLS SHIP DISABLED AND flow.js ENABLES THEM. Without that, a
          reader with JavaScript off could flip a switch and drag the slider —
          both are native controls and both move on their own — while none of
          the numbers underneath them changed. The card would then be showing
          "catalog off, forty products" above "€85 a product, 12 products, 84
          files", which is not a stale picture but a false one. Disabled, the
          card is what the header promises: a true, fixed picture of one real
          order. See enable() in src/scripts/flow.js. */}
      <div class="fl-card">
        <ul class="fl-scopes">
          {scopes.map((s) => (
            <li class="fl-scope">
              <label class="fl-toggle">
                <input type="checkbox" data-flow-scope={s.id} checked={s.on !== false} disabled />
                <span class="fl-switch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <span class="fl-scope-text">
                  <span class="fl-scope-t">{s.t}</span>
                  <span class="fl-scope-b">{s.b}</span>
                </span>
              </label>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>

        <div class="fl-count">
          <label class="fl-count-label" for="fl-count">{countLabel}</label>
          <output class="fl-count-out" for="fl-count" data-flow-count>{startCount}</output>
          <input
            class="fl-range"
            id="fl-count"
            type="range"
            min="1"
            max={maxCount}
            step="1"
            value={startCount}
            data-flow-range
            disabled
          />
          <span class="fl-count-unit">{countUnit}</span>
        </div>

        {/* The running total. aria-live so a screen-reader user dragging the
            slider hears the number change; polite, because it changes on every
            step and assertive would interrupt continuously.

            It HIDES rather than blanks when every switch is off. A price line
            standing empty reads as a number that failed to load; an absent one
            reads as nothing to price, which is what it is. */}
        <p class="fl-price" aria-live="polite" data-flow-price hidden={!startPriced}>
          <span class="fl-price-rate" data-flow-rate>{start.rate}</span>
          <span class="fl-price-per" data-flow-per>{start.per}</span>
          <span class="fl-price-total" data-flow-total>{start.total}</span>
          <span class="fl-price-vat">{vat}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="fl-empty" data-flow-empty hidden={startPriced}>{emptyLabel}</p>
        {/* The label is a visually-hidden span, not an aria-label on the <p>.
            `role=paragraph` is in ARIA's name-prohibited set, so the attribute
            was either dropped — leaving flowNextLabel as dead copy in both
            languages — or, where a browser did honour it, it REPLACED the
            sentence carrying the actual number. A span is read in sequence
            with it instead of instead of it. */}
        <p class="fl-next" data-flow-next hidden={!start.next}>
          <span class="fl-sr">{nextLabel}: </span><span data-flow-next-text>{start.next}</span>
        </p>
      </div>
      {/* The count of what leaves the studio. It rendered as a bare "84" with
          no word beside it in the first pass — a number floating under a card,
          which is the kind of thing that reads as a bug rather than a fact.
          The page supplies the sentence with a {n} hole in it so the word order
          can differ per language; splitting on the hole keeps the number in its
          own element for the script to write to. */}
      <p class="fl-images">
        {imagesBefore}<strong data-flow-images>{startImages * startCount}</strong>{imagesAfter}
      </p>
    </section>

    <span class="fl-flow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fl-token"></span></span>

    {/* ── 3 · THE GATE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        Two uprights with the rail between them, and the refusing side drawn
        beside the passing one. FigCapacity's rule holds: the two tracks carry
        no scale, no ticks and no bands to count. The claim is "this can say
        no", not "here is how full next week is". */}
    <section class="fl-stage" data-flow-stage="gate">
      <h3 class="fl-h">{stages.gate}</h3>
      <div class="fl-gate">
        <span class="fl-post" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div class="fl-gate-body">
          <p class="fl-gate-pass"><span class="fl-tick" aria-hidden="true"></span>{gatePass}</p>
          <p class="fl-gate-timing" data-flow-timing>{startTiming}</p>
          <p class="fl-gate-refuse"><span class="fl-cross" aria-hidden="true"></span>{gateRefuse}</p>
        </div>
        <span class="fl-post" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="fl-sub">{gateNote}</p>
    </section>

    <span class="fl-flow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fl-token"></span></span>

    {/* ── 4 · PRODUCTION AND THE HUMAN CHECK ────────────────────────────
        Two nodes, not one. The second is the whole difference between this and
        a prompt box, and folding it into "we produce" is how it disappears. */}
    <section class="fl-stage" data-flow-stage="make">
      <h3 class="fl-h">{stages.make}</h3>
      <ol class="fl-make">
        {makeSteps.map((m) => (
          <li class="fl-make-step">
            <span class="fl-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="fl-make-t">{m.t}</span>
            <span class="fl-make-b">{m.b}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>

    <span class="fl-flow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="fl-token"></span></span>

    {/* ── 5 · DELIVERY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        FigApproval's parallelism, kept: three tiles in three states at once.
        The tiles are aria-hidden and the legend carries the meaning in text,
        exactly as FigApproval did — a decorative grid that a screen reader
        walks through tile by tile is noise, and the states are the content. */}
    <section class="fl-stage" data-flow-stage="deliver">
      <h3 class="fl-h">{stages.deliver}</h3>
      <p class="fl-delivery" data-flow-delivery>{startDelivery}</p>
      <div class="fl-portal" data-flow-portal hidden={!startAttended}>
        <ul class="fl-tiles" aria-hidden="true">
          <li class="fl-tile is-ok"></li>
          <li class="fl-tile is-rev"></li>
          <li class="fl-tile is-wait"></li>
          <li class="fl-tile is-ok"></li>
          <li class="fl-tile is-wait"></li>
          <li class="fl-tile is-ok"></li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="fl-legend">
          <li><span class="fl-key is-ok" aria-hidden="true"></span>{portalStates.ok}</li>
          <li><span class="fl-key is-rev" aria-hidden="true"></span>{portalStates.rev}</li>
          <li><span class="fl-key is-wait" aria-hidden="true"></span>{portalStates.wait}</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ── 6 · THE FAN-OUT ───────────────────────────────────────────────
        The reference splits one € into three currencies curving down to three
        merchants. This splits one visual into the five shapes the channels
        ask for. Real `aspect-ratio` on every frame, because the caption
        promises each frame IS the ratio under it. */}
    <span class="fl-split" aria-hidden="true">
      {/* Five columns, so the endpoints are the five column CENTRES: at
          `repeat(5, 1fr)` those fall at 10 / 30 / 50 / 70 / 90 percent, which
          in this 500-unit box is 50 / 150 / 250 / 350 / 450. The first pass
          used 22 / 108 / 250 / 392 / 478 in a box narrower than the grid, so
          every line landed somewhere between two frames. The wrapper is now
          the same width as the grid it feeds, which is the only way these two
          stay aligned when the container resizes. */}
      <svg viewBox="0 0 500 90" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false">
        <path d="M250 0 V24 Q250 50 202 50 H98 Q50 50 50 76 V90" />
        <path d="M250 0 V24 Q250 50 226 50 H174 Q150 50 150 76 V90" />
        <path d="M250 0 V90" />
        <path d="M250 0 V24 Q250 50 274 50 H326 Q350 50 350 76 V90" />
        <path d="M250 0 V24 Q250 50 298 50 H402 Q450 50 450 76 V90" />
      </svg>
    </span>

    <ul class="fl-out">
      {channels.map((ch, i) => (
        <li class="fl-out-frame">
          <span class="fl-crop" style={`aspect-ratio:${RATIOS[i % RATIOS.length].r}`}>
            <span class="fl-crop-drawn" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="fl-crop-ratio">{RATIOS[i % RATIOS.length].label}</span>
          </span>
          <span class="fl-out-t">{ch.t}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figcaption class="fl-note">{note}</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     FigFlow

     TWO THINGS THIS FILE HAS TO GET RIGHT AND THE FIVE FIGURES DID NOT HAVE TO.

     1 · IT IS TALL. Five figures each got their own section with the page's
         rhythm around them. One rail is a single element roughly five screens
         high, so the vertical rhythm has to live INSIDE it — hence the stage
         padding rather than section margins.

     2 · IT MOVES. Every animated property here is transform or opacity, both
         compositor-only, and the script never writes a style — it adds one
         class. Nothing here can force layout while the page scrolls.

     THE DEFAULT IS LIT. `.fl-stage` is fully visible with no classes on it. The
     script ADDS `.is-lit` as each stage arrives; the dimming only exists once
     `.js` is on <html> and motion is allowed. Same contract as `.reveal
     pending` — see global.css 1992. Get this backwards and the figure is blank
     for anyone whose JS fails.
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

  .fl { margin: 0; }

  .fl-rail {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* Measured, then cut. The first pass used clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.6rem) top and
     bottom on every stage PLUS a 5rem connector between them, which put about
     nine rem of nothing between two things the reader is meant to see as one
